[Term]
id: HP:0011504
name: Bull's eye maculopathy
def: "Progressive maculopathy characterized by concentric regions of hyper- and hypopigmentation, with an initial foveal sparing and whose appearance is said to resemble the central target of a dart board." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592, PMID:31704701]
is_a: HP:0008002 ! Abnormal macular pigmentation
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T08:57:53Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011505
name: Cystoid macular edema
def: "Cystoid thickening of the retina that takes place due to accumulation of extracellular fluid in the macula as a nonspecific response to blood-retinal barrier breakdown. Histological studies show that radially orientated cystoid spaces consisting of ophthalmoscopically clear fluid are often clinically detectable in the macula area." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter, PMID:19668445]
comment: Macular edema is a common pathological response to a wide variety of ocular insults, most commonly after intraocular (e.g. cataract) surgery or in association with retinal vascular (e.g. diabetic eye disease, retinal vein occlusion) or inflammatory (e.g. uveitis) disease.
is_a: HP:0040049 ! Macular edema
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:12:59Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011506
name: Choroidal neovascularization
def: "Choroidal neovascularization (CNV) is the inward growth of new blood vessels arising from the choriocapillaris. Depending on the stage of development, they can be external (type 1 NV) or internal (type 2 NV) to the retinal pigment epithelium." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter]
comment: Choroidal neovascularization can be extrafoveal, subfoveal, or juxtafoveal.
synonym: "Choroidal neovascular membrane" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0000610 ! Abnormal choroid morphology
is_a: HP:0001103 ! Abnormal macular morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:28:46Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011507
name: Macular flecks
def: "Pale, often indistinct, multiple lesions of the macula, of variable shape that is not round. The lesions can be notched, angular, or pisciform (said to resemble a fish)." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
comment: Macular flecks can be seen in Stargardt disease as well as several types of pattern dystrophy.
is_a: HP:0030500 ! Yellow/white macular lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:38:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011508
name: Macular hole
def: "A macular hole is a full thickness gap in the macula, located in the center of the retina." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter]
comment: Full-thickness macular hole (FTMH) is defined as a foveal lesion with interruption of all retinal layers from the internal limiting membrane to the retinal pigment epithelium. Full-thickness macular hole is primary if caused by vitreous traction or secondary if directly the result of pathologic characteristics other than vitreomacular traction. Full-thickness macular hole is subclassified by size of the hole as determined by OCT and the presence or absence of vitreomacular traction.
is_a: HP:0001103 ! Abnormal macular morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:39:58Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011509
name: Macular hyperpigmentation
def: "Increased amount of pigmentation in the macula." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter]
is_a: HP:0008002 ! Abnormal macular pigmentation
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:52:11Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011510
name: Drusen
def: "Drusen (singular, 'druse') are tiny yellow or white accumulations of extracellular material (lipofuscin) that build up in the Bruch membrane of the eye." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
is_a: HP:0030506 ! Yellow/white retinal lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:54:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011511
name: Macular schisis
def: "Splitting of the retina in the macular region." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
comment: This abnormality can be seen in genetic conditions, such as X-linked retinoschisis and retinitis pigmentosa, and more diffusely in autosomal recessive bestrophinopathy. It can also be tractional or idiopathic.
is_a: HP:0030502 ! Retinoschisis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T09:55:43Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011512
name: Fundus hyperpigmentation
def: "Increased pigmentation of the fundus" [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter, https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0986-4123]
synonym: "Hyperpigmentation of the fundus" EXACT []
synonym: "Hyperpigmented fundi" EXACT []
synonym: "Hyperpigmented fundus" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031605 ! Abnormal fundus pigmentation
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T10:34:11Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011513
name: Retinal cavernous hemangioma
alt_id: HP:0030508
def: "A benign tumor of the retina that appears as a grouping of blood-filled saccules within the inner retinal layers or on the surface of the optic disc. Retinal cavernous hemangioma are described as having a 'cluster of grapes' appearance." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199, PMID:20844673]
comment: The lesions are variable in size and location, and frequently follow the course of a major retinal vein. Epiretinal membranes are a common feature. In contrast to retinal capillary hemangiomas, cavernous hemangiomas lack prominent feeder vessels, intraretinal exudate, and surrounding subretinal fluid. Because the fundoscopic features are characteristic, diagnosis can generally be made based upon clinical appearance. These hemangiomas can be associated with cavernous brain hemangiomas. The use of "Retinal cavernous angioma" is not recommended, as strictly speaking only hemangiomas occur in the retina.
synonym: "Retinal cavernous angioma" EXACT []
synonym: "Retinal cavernous haemangioma" EXACT uk_spelling []
is_a: HP:0007797 ! Developmental retinal vascular malformation
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T04:12:54Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011514
name: Abnormality of binocular vision
def: "An abnormality of binocular vision, that is of the ability to synthesize the visual inputs from both eyes to a single image with perception of depth." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter]
is_a: HP:0000504 ! Abnormality of vision
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:00:34Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011515
name: Abnormal stereopsis
def: "Inability to make fine depth discriminations from parallax provided by the two eyes' different positions on the head." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
is_a: HP:0011514 ! Abnormality of binocular vision
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:03:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011516
name: Achromatopsia
def: "A condition where the retina contains no functional cone cells, so that in addition to the absence of color discrimination, vision in lights of normal intensity is difficult." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592, PMID:12015282]
comment: The end stage of cone degeneration is rod monochromacy, also called achromatopsia, in which vision is mediated exclusively by rod photoreceptors. Rod monochromacy can also be manifest from birth and can be due to congenitally absent or dysfunctional cone photoreceptors. There is no rod counterpart to cone degeneration; all rod photoreceptor degenerations have a secondary loss of cones and are categorized as retinitis pigmentosa.
synonym: "Rod monochromacy" EXACT []
synonym: "Rod monochromatism" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0007803 ! Monochromacy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:40:03Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011517
name: Cone monochromacy
def: "The condition of having both rods and cones, but only a single kind of cone. Affected individuals have good pattern vision in daylight, but cannot distinguish between colors." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_ncarter]
comment: There are three differing forms of cone monochromacy, named according to the residual functioning cone class, (1) blue cone (S-cone) monochromacy, green cone (M-cone) monochromacy, and red cone (L-cone) monochromacy.
is_a: HP:0007803 ! Monochromacy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:40:33Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011518
name: Dichromacy
def: "Individuals affected by dichromacy possess only two types of cones, instead of three." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
comment: The three types of dichromacy (protanopia, deuteranopia, and tritanopia) are named according the cone photopigment that is missing.
is_a: HP:0007641 ! Dyschromatopsia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:46:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011519
name: Anomalous trichromacy
def: "Individuals with anomalous trichromacy possess three types of cones, but one of the three types of cones has an abnormal spectral sensitivity compared to normal cones." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
comment: Because the spectral sensitivities of individuals with anomalous trichromacy are shifted compared to normal, they mix the primary colors in different proportions.
is_a: HP:0007641 ! Dyschromatopsia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T06:57:10Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011520
name: Deuteranomaly
def: "A type of anomalous trichromacy associated with abnormal M photopigment, such that the absorption spectrum is shifted toward L wavelengths. Affected individuals have difficulties distinguishing between red and green." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
comment: Note that protanomaly and deuteranomaly are both associated with difficulties distinguishing red and green.
synonym: "Deuteranomoly" EXACT []
synonym: "Green-weak" EXACT layperson [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6908-9849]
is_a: HP:0000642 ! Red-green dyschromatopsia
is_a: HP:0011519 ! Anomalous trichromacy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T07:14:19Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011521
name: Deuteranopia
def: "Complete lack of the M photopigment, which is replaced with the L photopigment. Affected individuals tend to confuse red and green." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
comment: Blue and red cones only; no functional green cones.
synonym: "Green-blind" EXACT layperson [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6908-9849]
is_a: HP:0000642 ! Red-green dyschromatopsia
is_a: HP:0011518 ! Dichromacy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T07:34:40Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011522
name: Protanopia
def: "Blue and green cones only; no functional red cones." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8727-6592]
synonym: "Red-blind" EXACT layperson [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6908-9849]
is_a: HP:0000642 ! Red-green dyschromatopsia
is_a: HP:0011518 ! Dichromacy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-06T07:45:06Z" xsd:dateTime

