[Term]
id: HP:0011582
name: Abdominal ectopia cordis
def: "Displacement of the heart outside the thoracic cavity and into the abdomen." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown]
is_a: HP:0001683 ! Ectopia cordis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:10:32Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011583
name: Cervical ectopia cordis
def: "A type of ectopia cordis with the heart partially in the cervical region and without a defect of the sternum." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
comment: This type of ectopia cordis presumably represents a retention of the heart in its embryonic position in the neck.
is_a: HP:0001683 ! Ectopia cordis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:11:40Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011584
name: Thoracocervical ectopia cordis
def: "A type of ectopia cordis with the heart partially in the cervical region with a defect of the superior portion of the sternum." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
is_a: HP:0001683 ! Ectopia cordis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:12:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011585
name: Thoracic ectopia cordis
def: "Congenital malformation of the thoracic wall with partial or total displacement of the heart outside the thoracic cavity. This feature is associated with sternal cleft or absence of the sternum." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
is_a: HP:0001683 ! Ectopia cordis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:13:18Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011586
name: Thoracoabdominal ectopia cordis
def: "Congenital malformation of the ventral wall with partial or total evisceration of the heart outside the thoracic cavity and displacement partially into the abdominal cavity." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199, PMID:19479716]
comment: In many cases, thoracicoabdominal ectopia cordis is characterized by partial absence or cleft of the sternum, deficiency of the anterior diaphragm, deficiency of the diaphragmatic pericardium, midline abdominal wall defect, and congenital intracardiac abnormalities. These five characteristics comprise the pentalogy of Cantrell.
is_a: HP:0001683 ! Ectopia cordis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:13:52Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011587
name: Abnormal branching pattern of the aortic arch
def: "A deviance from the norm of the origin or course of the right brachiocephalic artery, the left common carotid artery, the left subclavian artery or the proximal vertebral arteries." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown]
comment: The normal aortic arch crosses the left mainstem bronchus and descends in the left paravertebral gutter. The coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses. The first branch of the aorta is normally the right brachiocephalic artery, then the left common carotid artery, then the left subclavian artery. Abnormal branching refers to any deviance from the norm of the origin or course of these branches or the proximal vertebral arteries. An anomaly of the aortic arch that results in complete encirclement of the trachea and esophagus by vascular structures is a vascular ring.
is_a: HP:0012303 ! Abnormal aortic arch morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:34:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011588
name: Cervical aortic arch
def: "The aortic arch extends into the soft tissues of the neck before turning down into to become the descending aorta." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown]
is_a: HP:0011587 ! Abnormal branching pattern of the aortic arch
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:37:51Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011589
name: Common origin of the right brachiocephalic artery and left common carotid artery
def: "The left common carotid artery has a common origin with the innominate artery." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199, PMID:17138027]
comment: Commonly the three great vessels (innominate artery, left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery) originate from the arch of the aorta. The second most common variant of aortic arch branching occurs when the left common carotid artery has a common origin with the innominate artery.
synonym: "Bovine arch" RELATED []
synonym: "Common brachiocephalic trunk" EXACT []
synonym: "Ovine arch" RELATED []
is_a: HP:0011587 ! Abnormal branching pattern of the aortic arch
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:38:36Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0011590
name: Double aortic arch
def: "A conenital abnormality of the aortic arch in which the two embryonic aortc arches form a vascular ring that surrounds the trachea or esophagus and then join to form the descending aorta. Double aortic arch can cause symptoms because of compression of the esophagus (dysphagia, cyanosis while eating) or trachea (stridor)." [HPO_CONTRIBUTOR:DDD_dbrown, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199, PMID:15148283, PMID:15564538]
xref: Fyler:2761
is_a: HP:0011587 ! Abnormal branching pattern of the aortic arch
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2012-04-08T01:46:10Z" xsd:dateTime

