def: "An abnormal concentration or amount of a mineral in the urine. Medically relevant minerals include calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sodium, chloride, magnesium, iron, zinc, iodine, chromium, copper, fluoride, molybdenum, manganese, and selenium." []
is_a: HP:0003110 ! Abnormality of urine homeostasis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-04-26T18:13:40Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025641
name: Elevated circulating glycolate concentration
def: "The concentration of glycolate in the blood circulation is above the upper limit of normal." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9969-8610, PMID:2002628]
comment: Glycolate is a hydroxy monocarboxylic acid anion that is acetate in which the methyl group has been hydroxylated.
is_a: HP:0010996 ! Abnormal circulating monocarboxylic acid concentration
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-04-26T18:31:31Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025643
name: Tarlov cyst
def: "A cerebrospinal fluid-filled nerve root cyst most often localized in the sacral spine." [PMID:26435653]
synonym: "Perineural cyst" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0030724 ! Central nervous system cyst
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-07-24T09:26:34Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025644
name: Fixation-off epileptiform discharges
def: "Fixation off sensitivity (FOS) is characterized by posterior or generalized epileptiform discharges that consistently occur with conditions that eliminate central vision, such as closed eyes, complete darkness, modified Ganzfeld stimulation (using a large white surface without visual cues), and Frenzel lenses. The FOS electroencephalogram (EEG) pattern usually consists of spikes/polyspikes and waves localized in occipital regions (bilateral or unilateral) or generalized discharges. It is usually inhibited by fixation of gaze, observing patterns, or intermittent photic stimulation (e.g., flashing light or patterns used as stimuli during the recording of the EEG)." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178, PMID:22925838, PMID:24099056]
comment: FOS occurs mainly in children who have non-acquired focal epilepsies with occipital epileptiform discharges and in people with generalized epilepsies with photosensitivity such as eyelid myoclonia with absences. It has been observed in people with epilepsies due to structural brain lesions and people without epilepsy who have posterior epileptiform discharges.
synonym: "Fixation-off sensitivity" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0011182 ! Interictal epileptiform activity
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-30T15:42:07Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025646
name: Bilateral polymicrogyria
def: "Polymicrogyria that affects all or some of both cerebral hemispheres." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178]
comment: Bilateral polymicrogyria is not always generalized or perisylvian.
is_a: HP:0002126 ! Polymicrogyria
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-30T15:42:07Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025647
name: Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome
def: "A type of nephrotic syndrome in which complete remission (albumin urine dipstick negative/trace or proteinuria <4mg/m2/d or urinary protein to creatinine ratio <200mg/g (<20 mg/mmol) for 3 consecutive days) is achieved within initial 4 weeks of corticosteroid therapy." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2234-4248, PMID:29910038]
synonym: "SSNS" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0000100 ! Nephrotic syndrome
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-04-02T19:27:44Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025648
name: Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome with infrequent relapses
def: "A type of steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome in which relapses occur at a frequency of one relapse (albumin urine dipstick at least 3+ or proteinuria over 40 mg/m2/d or urinary protein to creatinine ratio at least 2000 mg/g for 3 consecutive days) within 6 months of initial response, or one to three relapses in any 12-month period." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2234-4248, PMID:29910038]
comment: No biopsy needed. Relapses are treated with steroids according to local protocols.
is_a: HP:0025647 ! Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-04-02T19:27:44Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025649
name: Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome with frequent relapses
def: "A type of steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome in which relapses occur at a frequency of two or more relapses (albumin urine dipstick at least 3+ or proteinuria over 40 mg/m2/d or urinary protein to creatinine ratio at least 2000 mg/g for 3 consecutive days) within 6 months of initial response or four or more relapses in any 12-month period." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2234-4248, PMID:29910038]
comment: Biopsy is necessary to determine underlying histology. Treatment with levamisole or immunosuppression (e.g. calcineurin inhibitors, cyclophosphamide or mycophenolat mofetil) in order to reduce steroid toxicity.
is_a: HP:0025647 ! Steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-04-02T19:27:44Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025650
name: Steroid-dependent nephrotic syndrome
def: "A type of nephrotic syndrome in which two consecutive relapses (albumin urine dipstick at least 3+ or proteinuria greater than 40 mg/m2/d or urinary protein to creatinine ratio at least 2000 mg/g for 3 consecutive days) occur during corticosteroid therapy, or within 14 days of ceasing therapy." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2234-4248, PMID:29910038]
comment: Biopsy is necessary to determine underlying histology. Treatment with levamisole or immunosuppression (e.g. calcineurin inhibitors, cyclophosphamide or mycophenolat mofetil) in order to reduce steroid toxicity.
synonym: "SDNS" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0000100 ! Nephrotic syndrome
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-04-02T19:27:44Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025652
name: Abnormal placental adhesion into the uterine wall
def: "Abnormal placental adhesion is characterized by an anomalous adherence of the placenta to the uterine wall. Based on the degree of adhesion, placental invasion can be classified into accreta, increta, or percreta." [PMID:30210239]
is_a: HP:0100767 ! Abnormal placenta morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-10-20T19:30:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025653
name: Placenta percreta
def: "Placenta percreta is a rare form of morbidly adherent placenta with penetration through the myometrium reaching serosa and even adjacent pelvic organs." [PMID:30210239]
synonym: "Abnormal placental pentration beyond the uterine wall" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0025652 ! Abnormal placental adhesion into the uterine wall
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-10-20T19:30:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025654
name: Placenta accreta
def: "Abnormally firm adherence of the placenta to the uterine wall." [PMID:15145367]
is_a: HP:0025652 ! Abnormal placental adhesion into the uterine wall
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-10-20T19:30:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025655
name: Placenta increta
def: "Abnormal placental pentration into but not beyond the uterine wall." []
is_a: HP:0025652 ! Abnormal placental adhesion into the uterine wall
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-10-20T19:30:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0025656
name: Prenatal double bubble sign
def: "Sonographic detection of a double bubble sign in the upper abdomen is strongly indicative of duodenal obstruction. One bubble represents fetal stomach, and the other is attributed to a dilated proximal part of the duodenum; continuity between both bubbles is required for the sign." [PMID:31167209]
is_a: HP:0034207 ! Abnormal fetal gastrointestinal system morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-10-20T19:30:09Z" xsd:dateTime

