property_value: terms:date "2017-10-15T13:19:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031593
name: Abnormal PR interval
def: "An anomaly of the PR interval, which is the portion of the ECG from the onset of the P wave to the beginning of the QRS complex. A normal PR interval in adults is 0.12-0.2 seconds." [PMID:23677846]
comment: The PR interval begins at the onset of the P wave and ends at the onset of the QRS complex. This interval reflects the time the impulse takes to reach the ventricles from the sinus node. In contrast, the PR segment begins at the endpoint of the P wave and ends at the onset of the QRS complex. It reflects the conduction from the atrioventricular node, down the bundle of His and through the bundle branches to the muscle.
is_a: HP:0003115 ! Abnormal EKG
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-10-15T13:27:21Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031594
name: PR segment depression
def: "A reduction in voltage of the PR segment below baseline." []
comment: The differential diagnosis includes normal variant, pericarditis or atrial infarct.
synonym: "PR interval depression" EXACT []
synonym: "PTa depression" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031596 ! Abnormal PR segment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-10-15T13:31:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031595
name: Abnormal P wave
def: "Any anomaly of the P wave of the EKG, which results from atrial depolarization. The P wave occurs when the sinoatrial node creates an action potential that depolarizes the atria." []
comment: P waves are normally upright in leads I and II and inverted in aVR.
is_a: HP:0003115 ! Abnormal EKG
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-10-15T13:31:59Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031596
name: Abnormal PR segment
def: "An anomaly of the PR segment, which begins at the endpoint of the P wave and ends at the onset of the QRS complex. The PR segment is normally flat and isoelectric." []
comment: The PR interval begins at the onset of the P wave and ends at the onset of the QRS complex. This interval reflects the time the impulse takes to reach the ventricles from the sinus node. In contrast, the PR segment begins at the endpoint of the P wave and ends at the onset of the QRS complex. It reflects the conduction from the atrioventricular node, down the bundle of His and through the bundle branches to the muscle.
is_a: HP:0003115 ! Abnormal EKG
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-10-15T13:35:56Z" xsd:dateTime

