property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T15:32:01Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031656
name: Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve
def: "Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve (SAM) is a paradoxical motion of the anterior, and occasionally posterior, mitral valve leaflet towards the left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) during systole." [PMID:22847594]
is_a: HP:0031481 ! Abnormal mitral valve physiology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T15:49:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031657
name: Abnormal heart sound
def: "Any abnormal noise generated by the beating heart." []
comment: The first two heart sounds, S1 and S2, define ventricular systole. S1 comprises the closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves. S2 is produced by aortic and pulmonic valve closure.
is_a: HP:0011025 ! Abnormal cardiovascular system physiology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T15:56:24Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031658
name: Third heart sound
def: "The third heart sound (S3) is related to rapid filling in diastole. S3 can be a normal finding in children and adolescents but suggests heart failure in older patients." []
synonym: "S3" EXACT abbreviation []
synonym: "Ventricular gallop" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033113 ! Gallop rhythm
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:01:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031659
name: Fourth heart sound
def: "The fourth heart sound (S4) is a low-pitched sound that occurs just before the first heart sound (S1) when the atria contract to force blood into the left ventricle, that is, coincident with late diastolic filling of the ventricle due to atrial contraction." []
comment: S4 occurs in late diastole, is associated with atrial contraction, and coincides with the P wave on the EKG.
synonym: "Atrial gallop" EXACT []
synonym: "Presystolic gallop" EXACT []
synonym: "S4" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0033113 ! Gallop rhythm
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:03:22Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031660
name: Loud first heart sound
def: "Abnormally increased volume of the first heart sound." []
comment: S1 may be loud in early phases of rheumatic mitral stenosis.
is_a: HP:0031657 ! Abnormal heart sound
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:05:06Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031661
name: Abnormal second heart sound
def: "Any anomaly of the second heart sound (S2), which is produced by aortic (A2) and pulmonic (P2) valve closure. The A2-P2 interval normally increases with inspiration and narrows with expiration." []
is_a: HP:0031657 ! Abnormal heart sound
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:06:41Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031662
name: Fixed splitting of the second heart sound
def: "Lack of variation in the splitting between the two components of the second heart sound with respiration. Normally, the aortic valve closure (A2) is followed by the pulmonic valve closure (P2) but the A2-P2 interval increases with inspiration and decreases with expiration." []
comment: Fixed splitting of S2 may occur in patients with secundum atrial septal defect.
synonym: "Fixed splitting of S2" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0031661 ! Abnormal second heart sound
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:08:14Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031663
name: Paradoxical splitting of the second heart sound
def: "Normally, the aortic valve closure (A2) is followed by the pulmonic valve closure (P2) but the A2-P2 interval increases with inspiration and decreases with expiration. With paradoxical splitting, there is a delay in the closure of the aortic valve, so that A2 can follow P2; the individual components can be appreciated at the end of expiration and the interval narrows with inspiration (which is the opposite of the normal pattern)." []
synonym: "Paradoxical splitting of S2" EXACT abbreviation []
synonym: "Reversed splitting of S2" EXACT abbreviation []
synonym: "Reversed splitting of the second heart sound" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031661 ! Abnormal second heart sound
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:11:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031664
name: Systolic heart murmur
def: "A heart murmur limited to systole, i.e., between the first and second heart sounds S1 and S2." []
is_a: HP:0030148 ! Heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:16:41Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031665
name: Midsystolic murmur
def: "A systolic murmur that begins after S1 and ends before S2, typically with a crescendo-decrescendo pattern." []
comment: A common cause of midsystolic murmurs in adults is aortic stenosis.
is_a: HP:0031664 ! Systolic heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:17:45Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031666
name: Late systolic murmur
def: "A murmur that occurs in the latter phase of systole." []
comment: A late systolic murmur heard best at the apex of the heart may indicate mitral valve prolapse.
is_a: HP:0031664 ! Systolic heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:19:04Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031667
name: Holosystolic murmur
def: "A heart murmur that occurs during the entire systolic phase from S1 to S2." []
is_a: HP:0031664 ! Systolic heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:20:33Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031668
name: Diastolic heart murmur
def: "A heart murmur that occurs during diastole, i.e., in the time between S2 and the subsequent S1." []
comment: Diastolic murmurs always signify structural heart disease.
is_a: HP:0030148 ! Heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:21:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031669
name: Middiastolic murmur
def: "A murmur that occurs in the middle of the diastolic phase." []
comment: A middiastolic murmur may indicate mitral stenosis.
is_a: HP:0031668 ! Diastolic heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:22:29Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031670
name: Continuous heart murmur
def: "A murmur that occurs in both systole and diastole." []
comment: Continuous murmurs are related to pressure gradients that persist across systole and diastole. A classic example is the murmur related to patent ductus arteriosus.
is_a: HP:0030148 ! Heart murmur
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:23:25Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031671
name: Typical atrial flutter
def: "Typical atrial flutter is an organized atrial tachycardia. It can also be defined as a macroreentrant tachycardia confined to the right atrium. This arrhythmia has a 200-260 ms cycle length, although it may fluctuate depending on patient's previous treatment or ablation, congenital heart disease, etc. Ventricular rate response will be limited by the atrioventricular node conductions, usually presenting a 2:1 or 3:1 response, during atrial flutter. Typical (counter clockwise) flutter is associated with the common flutter pattern: a regular continuous undulation with dominant negative deflections in inferior leads II, III and aVF, often described also as a saw tooth pattern, and flat atrial deflections in leads I and aVL. Atrial deflections in V1 can be positive, biphasic or negative." [PMID:28835836]
is_a: HP:0004749 ! Atrial flutter
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:44:25Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031672
name: Reverse typical atrial flutter
def: "A type of atrial flutter associated with rounded or bimodal positive deflections in inferior leads II, III and aVF, and a very characteristic bimodal negative wave in the shape of a W is seen in lead V1." [PMID:28835836]
is_a: HP:0004749 ! Atrial flutter
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:47:28Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031673
name: Orthodromic atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia
def: "A type of atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia (AVRT) where the atrioventricular node is used for anterograde conduction and the accessory pathway for retrograde conduction." [PMID:29234357]
synonym: "Orthodromic AVRT" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0011717 ! Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:55:02Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031674
name: Antidromic atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia
def: "A type of atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia (AVRT) where the accessory pathway is used for anterograde conduction and the atrioventricular node for retrograde conduction." []
synonym: "Antidromic AVRT" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0011717 ! Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T16:57:22Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031675
name: Fascicular left ventricular tachycardia
def: "A ventricular tachycardia (VT) characterized by right bundle branch block (RBBB) and left axis deviation (LAD) on electrocardiogram (ECG)." [PMID:29184614]
comment: Fascicular left ventricular tachycardia occurs predominantly in young males (15-40 years old) without structural heart disease (idiopathic).
is_a: HP:0004756 ! Ventricular tachycardia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:01:47Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031676
name: Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia
def: "A type of ventricular tachycardia that is characterized by uniform QRS complexes within each lead (i.e., each QRS is identical or nearly so)." []
comment: Monomorphic VT originates from a single ventricular focus.
is_a: HP:0004756 ! Ventricular tachycardia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:06:28Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031677
name: Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia
def: "A type of ventricular tachycardia that is characterized by variable QRS complexes within each lead (i.e., QRS complexes may be different from beat to beat)." []
is_a: HP:0004756 ! Ventricular tachycardia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:08:41Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031678
name: Atherosclerotic lesion
def: "A lesion associated with atherosclerosis, a multifactorial and multipart progressive disease manifested by the focal development within the arterial wall of lesions, that ranges from the development of a fatty streak, plaque progression, and plaque disruption. Atherosclerotic lesions demonstrate consistent morphological characteristics, which indicate that each type may stabilize temporarily or permanently and that progression to the next type may require an additional stimulus." [PMID:8172861]
is_a: HP:0002621 ! Atherosclerosis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:19:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031679
name: Type I atherosclerotic lesion
def: "Type I lesions represent the very initial changes and are recognized as an increase in the number of intimal macrophages and the appearance of macrophages filled with lipid droplets (foam cells)." []
comment: Type I lesions consist of the first microscopically and chemically detectable lipid deposits in the intima and the cell reactions associated with such deposits.The initial histological changes in the human intima are minimal. Small, isolated groups of macrophages containing lipid droplets (macrophage foam cells) form. In coronary arteries these cells pref- erentially accumulate in regions of the intima that have an adaptive intimal thickening of the eccentric type.
is_a: HP:0031678 ! Atherosclerotic lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:29:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031680
name: Type II atherosclerotic lesion
def: "Type II atherosclerotic lesions include the fatty streak lesion, the first grossly visible lesion, and are characterized by layers of macrophage foam cells and lipid droplets within intimal smooth muscle cells and minimal coarse-grained particles and heterogeneous droplets of extracellular lipid." [PMID:8172861]
is_a: HP:0031678 ! Atherosclerotic lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:31:10Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031681
name: Type III atherosclerotic lesion
def: "Type III (intermediate) atherosclerotic lesions are the morphological and chemical bridge between type II and advanced lesions. Type III lesions appear in some adaptive intimal thickenings (progression-prone locations) in young adults and are characterized by pools of extracellular lipid in addition to all the components of type II lesions." [PMID:8172861]
is_a: HP:0031678 ! Atherosclerotic lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:33:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031682
name: Type V atherosclerotic lesion
def: "Type V lesions are defined as lesions in which prominent new fibrous connective tissue has formed. When the new tissue is part of a lesion with a lipid core (type IV), this type of morphology may be referred to as fibroatheroma or type Va lesion. A type V lesion in which the lipid core and other parts of the lesion are calcified may be referred to as type Vb. A type V lesion in which a lipid core is absent and lipid in general is minimal may be referred to as type Vc. With these lesions, arteries are variously narrowed, generally more than with type IV. Importantly, as with type IV lesions, type V lesions may develop fissures, hematoma, and/or thrombus (type VI lesion), and for this reason too they are clinically relevant." []
is_a: HP:0031678 ! Atherosclerotic lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:38:22Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031683
name: Type VI atherosclerotic lesion
def: "Type VI atherosclerotic lesions generally have the underlying morphology of type IV or V lesions, surface disruptions, hematoma, and thrombosis may be (although less often) superimposed on any other type of lesion and even on intima without an apparent lesion. Complicating features may arise because of individual differences in risk factors and tissue reactions. These may include differences in composition of the blood, the relative quantities and distributions in the components of the underlying lesion or intima, as well as modifications of shear and tensile forces to which the lesion or intima is exposed. Clinical imaging of lesions may be expected to contribute greatly to the understanding of type VI lesions and the associated clinical syndromes." [PMID:7648691]
comment: Morbidity and mortality from atherosclerosis is largely due to type IV and type V lesions in which disruptions of the lesion surface, hematoma or hemorrhage, and thrombotic deposits have developed. Type IV or V lesions with one or more of these additional features are classified as type VI and may also be referred to as complicated lesions. Type VI may be subdivided by the superimposed features. Thus, disruption of the surface may be labeled type VIa; hematoma or hemorrhage, type VIb; and thrombosis, type VIc. Type VIabc indicates the presence of all three features.
is_a: HP:0031678 ! Atherosclerotic lesion
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T17:45:52Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031684
name: Renal artery atherosclerosis
def: "An atherosclerotic lesion located in the renal artery." []
is_a: HP:0008776 ! Abnormal renal artery morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T18:25:00Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031685
name: Abnormal stool composition
alt_id: HP:0032483
def: "Abnormal level of metabolite or other abnormal analyte result in a stool test." []
synonym: "Abnormal faecal test result" RELATED uk_spelling []
synonym: "Abnormal faeces composition" EXACT uk_spelling []
synonym: "Abnormal fecal test result" RELATED []
synonym: "Abnormal feces composition" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0001939 ! Abnormality of metabolism/homeostasis
is_a: HP:0025033 ! Abnormal digestive system morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T21:04:26Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031686
name: Increased stool alpha1-antitrypsin concentration
def: "An abnormally elevated amount of alpha1-antitrypsin in the feces." []
comment: Alpha-1-antitrypsin is resistant to degradation by digestive enzymes and can therefore be used as a marker for the presence of blood proteins in the intestinal tract (e.g., protein-losing enteropathy).
is_a: HP:0031685 ! Abnormal stool composition
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T21:09:20Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031687
name: Abnormally loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound
comment: The pulmonic component is considered to be abnormally loud in a subject over age 20 if it is greater than the aortic component in the second left ICS or if it is audible at the cardiac apex. This may be due either to pulmonary artery hypertension or right ventricular dilatation, with part of the right ventricle assuming the position normally occupied by the left ventricle. A split second sound at the apex is, therefore, definitely abnormal. The loud P2 commonly heard at the apex in patients with atrial septal defect is probably due to a dilated right ventricle encroaching upon the cardiac apex [source-NCBI:NBK341].
synonym: "Accentuation of the pulmonic component of the second heart sound" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031661 ! Abnormal second heart sound
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T21:17:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031688
name: Erythroid dysplasia
def: "Dysplasia in the erythroid lineage, which presents with a variety of morphological changes in the bone marrow, including nuclear budding or irregular nuclear contour in erythroblasts." [PMID:26791933]
synonym: "Dyserythropoiesis" RELATED []
is_a: HP:0012130 ! Abnormal erythroid lineage cell morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T22:04:44Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031689
name: Megakaryocyte dysplasia
def: "The presence of micro-megakaryocytes, hypo-lobed, or non-lobed nuclei in megakaryocytes of all sizes and multiple, widely-separated nuclei." [PMID:27127725]
synonym: "Dysmegakaryopoiesis" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0012143 ! Abnormal megakaryocyte morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T22:09:51Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031690
name: Opportunistic infection
def: "An infection that is caused by a pathogen that would generally not be able to cause an infection in a host with a normal immune system. Such pathogens take advantage of the opportunity, so to speak, that is provided by a weakened immune system." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199]
comment: Opportunistic infection are infections that occur more often or are more severe in people with weakened immune systems than in those with normal immune system functioning.
is_a: HP:0032101 ! Unusual infection
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T22:11:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031691
name: Severe viral infection
def: "An unusually severe viral infection." [PMID:21960712]
comment: The majority of viral infections acquired by nonimmunosuppressed individuals are asymptomatic or result in mild clinical manifestations. These individuals are able to mount effective immune responses that either clear the virus from the body or, for viruses that establish latency, maintain the virus in a dormant state. For individuals who are immunocompromised, because of a genetic immunodeficiency or immunosuppressive drug therapy, viral infections may result in life-threatening disease. Such patients present with infections that are severe, persistent, recurrent, or refractory to therapy and are usually caused by viruses of low virulence prevalent in the community.
is_a: HP:0032169 ! Severe infection
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T22:28:59Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031692
name: Severe cytomegalovirus infection
def: "An unusually severe infection by cytomegalovirus." [PMID:12588074]
comment: A typical mononucleosis syndrome consists of an acute febrile illness with an increase of 50 percent or more in the number of lymphocytes or monocytes, with at least 10 percent of the lymphocytes being atypical. Immunocompromised patients may experience more severe complications such as CMV retinitis.
is_a: HP:0031691 ! Severe viral infection
is_a: HP:5210146 ! Unusual cytomegalovirus infection
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5535-2845
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8124-8864
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2017-12-17T22:31:35Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031693
name: Severe Epstein Barr virus infection
def: "An unusually severe Epstein Barr virus (EBV) infection." []
synonym: "Fulminant infectious mononucleosis" EXACT []
synonym: "Severe EBV infection" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031691 ! Severe viral infection
is_a: HP:5210286 ! Unusual EBV infection
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5535-2845
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8124-8864
