[Term]
id: HP:0031850
name: Abnormal hematocrit
def: "Any deviation from the normal ratio of the volume of red blood cells to the total volume of blood." []
comment: Note that the hematocrit is one of the measurements that can contribution to the diagnosis of anemia or polycythemia, which is coded separately.
synonym: "Abnormal Hct" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0001877 ! Abnormal erythrocyte morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T15:58:22Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031851
name: Reduced hematocrit
def: "A reduction below the normal ratio of the volume of red blood cells to the total volume of blood." []
synonym: "Low hematocrit" EXACT []
synonym: "Reduced Hct" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0031850 ! Abnormal hematocrit
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T15:59:55Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031853
name: Isomerism
def: "Isomerism in the context of the congenitally malformed heart is defined as a situation where some paired structures on opposite sides of the left-right axis of the body are, in morphologic terms, symmetrical mirror images of each other." [PMID:21731561]
comment: The word isomerism is also derived from the Greek: iso-meaning equal and meros-meaning part. Isomerism refers to structures that are themselves mirror-imaged. When used in the area of the congenitally malformed heart, the term isomerism has become the conventional description for the situation in which morphologically right structures or morphologically left structures are found on both sides of the body in the same individual.
is_a: HP:0030853 ! Heterotaxy
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:12:17Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031854
name: Left Isomerism
def: "A type of heterotaxy where some paired structures on opposite sides of the left-right axis of the body are symmetrical mirror images of each other, and have the morphology of the normal left-sided structures." [PMID:21731561]
synonym: "Left-sided isomerism" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031853 ! Isomerism
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:13:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031855
name: Right isomerism
def: "A type of heterotaxy where some paired structures on opposite sides of the left-right axis of the body are symmetrical mirror images of each other, and have the morphology of the normal right-sided structures." [PMID:21731561]
synonym: "Right-sided isomerism" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0031853 ! Isomerism
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:15:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031856
name: Hobby horse gait
def: "An abnormal gait characterized by toe walking, stiff legs, and skipping. The gait pattern has some resemblance to cock-walk gait, but affected individuals are able to improve their dystonic gait by walking backward." [PMID:21956287]
is_a: HP:0031954 ! Dystonic gait
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:27:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031857
name: Ineffective esophageal peristalsis
def: "Reduced or inadequate esophageal peristalsis, with resultant slow passage of contents through the esophagus." [PMID:28665309]
comment: Ineffective esophageal motility can be diagnosed by high-resolution manometry if 50% or more swallows are ineffective (failed or weak with the distal contractile integral less than 450 mmHg/s/cm).
synonym: "Ineffective esophageal motility" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0025270 ! Abnormal esophagus physiology
is_a: HP:0100771 ! Hypoperistalsis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:42:33Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031858
name: Esophageal furrows
def: "Longitudinal grooves in the surface of the esophagus arranged in a longitudinal fashion (from top to bottom of the esophagus)." [PMID:25368745]
comment: Esophageal furrows may occur in inflammatory disorders of the esophagus such as eosinophilic esophagitis.
is_a: HP:0002031 ! Abnormal esophagus morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-05-05T18:45:29Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0031860
