[Term]
id: HP:0032160
name: Cryptococcal meningitis
def: "A type of fungal meningitis caused by an encapsulated yeast that belongs to the genus Cryptococcus. Cryptococcus neoformans and Cryptococcus gattii are responsible for the majority of cases of human cryptococcosis." [PMID:24715488]
comment: Cryptococcus yeasts are ubiquitous in the environment and are acquired by inhalation. In the immunocompromised host, reactivation of latent disease is believed to be responsible for the subsequent development of cryptococcal disease. For instance, in persons with depletion of CD4+ T cells, the ability of the immune response to adequately contain cryptococcal infection is impaired.
is_a: HP:0032159 ! Fungal meningitis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-12-09T12:26:39Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032161
name: Coccidioidal meningitis
def: "A type of fungal meningitis caused by dissemination of coccidioides to basilar meninges." [PMID:27927853]
comment: Coccidioides species are soil-dwelling fungi that are the causative pathogen of coccidioidomycosis, also known as Valley fever. Infection occurs from inhalation of fungal spores that become airborne from soil in endemic regions. Sixty percent of infections are asymptomatic. The rate of dissemination from primary infection to extrapulomonary sites is higher in immune-compromised individuals. A common site of dissemination is to the basilar meninges, resulting in coccidioidal meningitis.
is_a: HP:0032159 ! Fungal meningitis
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2018-12-09T12:30:16Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032162
name: Unusual skin infection
def: "Increased susceptibility to infection of the skin, nails, or mucous membranes, as manifested by recurrent or severe cutaneous or mucosal infection, or by skin or mucous membrane infection caused by an atypical or opportunistic organism." []
comment: Recurrent or unusual skin and mucous membrane infections are characteristic of T-cell deficiencies, phagocyte defects, and inborn errors of IL-17 immunity.
