[Term]
id: HP:0032536
name: Increased number of lymph nodes
def: "An abnormally elevated number of lymph nodes in an anatomical region." [PMID:20301630]
comment: For example, lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome is characterized by an increased number of lymphatic vessels and inguinal lymph nodes.
is_a: HP:0002733 ! Abnormal lymph node morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T13:15:53Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032537
name: Delayed fracture healing
def: "A delay in healing of a fracture past the expected duration." [PMID:24857030]
comment: Initially, after injury a hematoma forms and this is followed by inflammatory cells arriving at the fracture site to debride the wound. During the soft callus phase of healing a collar of bone forms at the periosteum adjacent to the fractured bone, and cells in the periosteum proliferate and differentiate into chondrocytes that form cartilage that bridges the fracture gap. During the hard callus phase, the cartilage is replaced by bone through endochondral ossification, possibly by transformation of some of the chondrocytes into osteoblasts. Finally, through remodeling, newly formed bone is restored to its pre-injured state. Depending on the site of the fracture, healing usually takes from 3 to 12 weeks in healthy adults.
is_a: HP:0011843 ! Abnormal musculoskeletal physiology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T13:23:40Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032538
name: Pretibial dimple
def: "A groove or crease on the shins (pretibial, i.e., over the shin bone). Pretibial creases may be obvious at birth and may range from 3 cm to over 15 cm in length and lengthen as the limb grows. They appear as an elongated dimple because of the attachment of skin to underlying tissue (e.g., to the tibia). The dimple or crease grows in proportion to the growth of the leg." [PMID:23444103]
is_a: HP:0010781 ! Skin dimple
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T13:33:38Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032539
name: Joint extensor surface localization
def: "Applies to an abnormality that is situated in extensor surface of the joint. The extensor surface refers to the skin on the opposite side of a joint." []
is_a: HP:0012838 ! Localized
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T13:48:37Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032540
name: Joint flexor surface localization
def: "Applies to an abnormality that is situated in flexor surface of the joint. The flexor surface refers to the skin that touches when a joint is bent (flexed)." []
is_a: HP:0012838 ! Localized
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T13:51:06Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032541
name: Knuckle pad
def: "Knuckle pads are benign fibrofatty subcutaneous pads located over the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints that can be mistaken for arthritis. Rarely they affect the dorsal aspect of the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints. Clinically they are painless and often affect both hands in an asymmetrical pattern." [PMID:26672439]
comment: Knuckle pads can be associated with camptodactyly (fixed flexion deformity of the interphalangeal joints of the little finger) as overlapping symptoms and are associated with many genetic factors. Although they can be associated to repetitive local trauma (for example repetitive batting of the knuckles, e.g. by boxers or suction the fingers by children), coexist with palmar (Dupuytren disease) or plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose disease) or Peyronie disease, most knuckle pads are idiopathic.
synonym: "DDN" EXACT abbreviation []
synonym: "Dorsal dupuytren nodules" EXACT []
synonym: "Knuckle pads on dorsal aspect of finger joints" EXACT plural_form []
is_a: HP:0011356 ! Regional abnormality of skin
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T15:11:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032542
name: Exacerbated by pregnancy
def: "Applied to a sign or symptom that is worsened by being pregnant." []
is_a: HP:0025285 ! Aggravated by
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T15:16:46Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032543
name: Lithoptysis
def: "Expectoration (coughing up) of a broncholith. Broncholithiasis is defined as the presence of calculi in the tracheobronchial tree. It is a rare disease but can be characterized by clinical and radiological findings of a calcified lymph node eroding bronchial wall and opening into the bronchial lumen." [PMID:23949189, PMID:29970776]
is_a: HP:0032016 ! Abnormal sputum
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-05-27T15:19:14Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032544
name: Predominant small joint localization
def: "Applies to an abnormality that mainly affects the small joints, including fingers, toes, interphalangeal, metacarpophalangeal, metatarsophalangeal, wrists, ankles, vertebrae, and neck." []
is_a: HP:0012838 ! Localized
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:19:53Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032545
name: Abdominal rigidity
def: "Involuntary tightening of the abdominal musculature that occurs in response to touching the abdomen to avoid pain. Rigidity can occur in the presence of abdominal inflammation and usually involves only the inflamed area." []
synonym: "Rigid abdomen" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002027 ! Abdominal pain
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:23:20Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032546
name: Abdominal guarding
def: "A voluntary contraction of the abdominal wall musculature to avoid pain." []
comment: Abdominal guarding tends to be generalized over the entire abdomen. In contrast to abdominal rigidity, guarding can often be overcome by having the patient purposely relax the muscles; rigidity cannot be.
is_a: HP:0002027 ! Abdominal pain
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:33:12Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032547
name: Low intraocular pressure
def: "An abnormal decrease of the pressure within the eye." [PMID:26253854]
comment: Ocular hypotony is defined statistically as an intraocular pressure (IOP) of less than 6.5 mmHg, three standard deviations below the mean. Clinically significant hypotony is when the IOP is so low that there is resultant visual loss. Hypotony has also been described as low pressure in an individual eye, leading to functional changes and structural changes.
synonym: "Ocular hypotony" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0012632 ! Abnormal intraocular pressure
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:37:45Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032548
name: Increased placental thickness
def: "Abnormally elevated placental thickness." [PMID:25077064]
comment: Placental thickness can be measured ultrasonographically at the thickest portion of the placenta or beneath the cord insertion, and in a representative portion perpendicular to the chorionic plate. The ultrasonographic measurement of placental thickness is a simple method to estimate placental size, and a thick placenta may be a useful predictor of adverse pregnancy outcomes.
synonym: "Placental thickness increased" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0012767 ! Abnormal placental size
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:49:00Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032549
name: Persistent asymmetrical tonic neck reflex
def: "Persistence beyond the normal age (roughly the first half of the first year of life) of the asymmetric tonic neck reflex (ATNR), which is an easily elicited primitive reflex in the immediate newborn period. The ATNR refers to the phenomenon whereby when the face of an infant is turned to one side, the ipsilateral arm and leg extend and the contralateral arm and leg flex. This posture has been compared to a typical posture of fencers." [PMID:441119]
synonym: "Persistent fencer's reflex" EXACT []
synonym: "Tonic neck reflex asymmetrical and persistent" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002476 ! Primitive reflex
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-12T10:56:12Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032550
name: Howell-Jolly bodies
def: "Howell-Jolly bodies are small, intra-erythrocytic remnants of erythrocyte nuclei. These inclusions are solitary in each erythrocyte and strongly basophilic. These are often confused with overlying platelets, but can be distinguished by the presence of a halo around overlying platelets." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9969-8610, PMID:21250106, PMID:26331020]
comment: Purple spheres, usually about 0.5 micrometers in diameter, presenting singly, or rarely multiply, in the cytoplasm of red blood cells.
is_a: HP:0020080 ! Erythrocyte inclusion bodies
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-14T10:19:19Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032551
name: Hemorrhoids
def: "Enlarged, bulging blood vessels in and around the anus often associated with rectal bleeding, itching, and pain." [PMID:21766771]
comment: Hemorrrhoids result from dilation of the submucosal vascular tissue in the distal anal canal. This vascular tissue is supported by connective tissue that, when weakened, leads to descent or prolapse of the hemorrhoids. Internal hemorrhoids originate above the dentate line (i.e., the junction between columnar and squamous epithelium) and are viscerally innervated and, therefore, painless. External hemorrhoids originate below the dentate line, have somatic innervation, and can cause pain. Some patients have both internal and external (mixed) hemorrhoids.
synonym: "Haemorrhoids" EXACT layperson []
synonym: "Piles" EXACT layperson []
is_a: HP:0002034 ! Abnormal rectum morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-06-14T10:37:17Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032552
name: Abnormal pulse
def: "An anomaly of the rhythmic throbbing of an artery that reflects the widening of the artery as blood flows through it and is caused by successive contractions of the heart." []
