[Term]
id: HP:0032576
name: Intracellular accumulation of Dol-PP-GlcNAc2Man5
def: "Intracellular accumulation of the lipid-linked oligosaccharide intermediate Man5GlcNAc2-PP-dolichol." [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5208-3432, PMID:10581255, PMID:16053906, PMID:28575298]
comment: Intracellular accumulation of Dol-PP-GlcNAc2Man5 can result from a defect in ALG3, which encodes a mannosyltransferase that transfers mannose from dolichyl phosphate mannose. The accumulation can be measured in fibroblasts. During protein N-glycosylation, glycans from lipid-linked oligosaccharides (LLOs) are transferred onto asparagine residues located in N-glycosylation sequons. The synthesis of LLO comprises three steps: It begins with the biosynthesis of the intermediate Dol-PP-GlcNAc2Man5, which occurs on the cytoplasmic side of the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). The second step is the flipping of this intermediate to the luminal side of the ER. The third step comprises the glycan extension in the ER lumen, yielding Dol-PP-GlcNAc2Man9.
is_a: HP:0011017 ! Abnormal cellular physiology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-12-14T09:45:01Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032577
name: Clonal T cell receptor rearrangement
def: "Presence of a predominant T cell clone. In PCR-based assays, this finding is inferred on the basis of one or two prominent bands within a valid size range. In NGS-based assays, this finding is inferred on the basis of a high number of reads that map to a single T cell receptor clone." [PMID:24688753]
is_a: HP:0025845 ! Abnormal TCR repertoire
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-12-14T09:59:49Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032578
name: Third ventricle colloid cyst
def: "An epithelial lined cyst filled with gelatinous material. The gelatinous material commonly contains mucin, old blood, cholesterol, and ions. Most colloid cysts identified are currently asymptomatic and identified incidentally on imaging. When a colloid cyst does cause issues, it most commonly causes obstructive hydrocephalus." [PMID:30542631]
comment: The colloid cyst is most commonly found in the rostral third ventricle at or near the foramen of Monro. The foramen of Monro is the conduit of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) outflow from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle. A colloid cyst can act as a ball valve, stopping CSF flow out of the lateral ventricles. If this occurs, CSF backs up into the lateral ventricles and causes ventriculomegaly and hydrocephalus.
is_a: HP:0010576 ! Intracranial cystic lesion
is_a: HP:0010951 ! Abnormal third ventricle morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2019-12-14T11:46:18Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0032579
