[Term]
id: HP:0033678
name: Acute coronary syndrome
def: "The term acute coronary syndrome (ACS) refers to any group of clinical symptoms compatible with acute myocardial ischemia and includes unstable angina (UA), non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)." [PMID:19797781]
comment: A diagnosis of NSTEMI can be made when the ischemia is sufficiently severe to cause myocardial damage that results in the release of a biomarker of myocardial necrosis into the circulation (cardiac-specific troponins T or I, or muscle and brain fraction of creatine kinase [CK-MB]). In contrast, the patient is considered to have experienced UA if no such biomarker can be detected in the bloodstream hours after the initial onset of ischemic chest pain. Unstable angina exhibits 1 or more of 3 principal presentations: (1) rest angina (usually lasting >20 minutes), (2) new-onset (<2 months previously) severe angina, and (3) a crescendo pattern of occurrence (increasing in intensity, duration, frequency, or any combination of these factors).
is_a: HP:0011025 ! Abnormal cardiovascular system physiology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-03T20:22:24Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033679
name: Abnormal red nucleus morphology
def: "Any structural anomaly of the red nucleus, a part of the midbrain involved in control of movement." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178]
is_a: HP:0002418 ! Abnormal midbrain morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-06T13:01:58Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033680
name: Pilocytic astrocytoma
def: "The most common form of astrocytoma (WHO Grade I) in childhood. These typically have MAPK signaling pathway abnormalities." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178, PMID:23817572]
xref: NCIT:C4047
is_a: HP:0009592 ! Astrocytoma
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-06T13:07:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033681
name: Oligodendroglioma
def: "Oligodendroglioma is a type of diffusely infiltrating glioma and constitutes approximately 5% of primary intracranial tumors. They often involve the cortical gray matter and are most commonly seen in the frontal lobes. OGs are generally low grade WHO grade II neoplasms that are slow-growing tumors and have a favorable treatment response when compared to other gliomas. Grade III anaplastic OG is a more malignant form of the tumor which portends a less favorable prognosis and may occur de novo or as degeneration from the lower grade OG." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178, PMID:32644610]
xref: NCIT:C3288
is_a: HP:0009733 ! Glioma
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-06T13:09:34Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033682
name: Pleomorphic xanthoastrocytoma
def: "Pleomorphic xanthoastrocytomas (PXA) are rare low-grade astrocytomas (WHO Grade II) typically found in the temporal lobe and classically presenting with epilepsy. PXA is an astrocytic neoplasm that most often presents in children or young adults but can also occur in adults. The diagnosis of anaplastic PXA is made based upon tumor histopathologic characteristics and requires increased proliferative activity (mitotic index at least 5 mitoses/10 HPF), which is associated with worse overall survival. In general, anaplastic PXAs acquire features of a more aggressive astrocytic neoplasm that can include increased proliferation, necrosis, microvascular proliferation, loss of pericellular reticulin, and increased infiltrative growth." [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178, PMID:30051528, PMID:31535562]
is_a: HP:0009592 ! Astrocytoma
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-06T13:14:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033683
name: Jaw hyperreflexia
def: "Increased intensity of muscle tendon reflexes in jaw." []
synonym: "Brisk jaw jerk" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0001347 ! Hyperreflexia
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-06T13:25:00Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033684
name: Abnormal muscle fiber-type distribution
def: "Ay deviation from the normal distribution of fiber types in skeletal muscle. The skeletal muscle groups of the mammalian body are made up of bundles of muscle fibers. These fibers can be assigned to different Types, with characteristic movement rates, response to neural inputs, and metabolic styles. Skeletal muscle fibers are broadly classified as slow-twitch (type 1) and fast-twitch (type 2). Multiple fiber types are generally intermingled within a single muscle group, and different muscle groups have varying proportions of fiber types" [PMID:27199166]
is_a: HP:0004303 ! Abnormal muscle fiber morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:37:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033685
name: Fiber type grouping
def: "An abnormal distribution of muscle fiber types in muscle tissue. Human skeletal muscle contains at least two fiber types recognizable by histochemical techniques. In transverse sections of normal skeletal muscle, type 1 and type 2 fibers are distributed in a random fashion. Grouping of fibers of the same type can be seen in certain peripheral neuropathies, thought to be due to reinnervation of denervated muscle fibers by sprouting axons. With grouping, motor units enlarge. The fibers of a motor unit, which are normally scattered, come to lie adjacent to one another. Histochemical examination shows groups of muscle fibers of the same histochemical type." [PMID:32715519]
synonym: "Fibre type grouping" EXACT uk_spelling []
is_a: HP:0033684 ! Abnormal muscle fiber-type distribution
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:40:37Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033686
name: Mitochondrial hypertrophy
def: "Enlargement of mitochondria. Mitochondrial hypertrophy is not discernible by light microscopy. By electron microscopy (EM), hypertrophic mitochondria have normal cristae and normal matrix density. In contrast, swollen mitochondria display swollen cristae and irregular matrix densities in EM." [PMID:32715519]
synonym: "Megamitochondria" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0012087 ! Abnormal mitochondrial shape
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:44:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033687
name: Short term memory impairment
def: "A deficit in the retention of pieces of information (memory chunks) for a relatively short time (usually up to 30 seconds)." [PMID:18394484, PMID:31424720]
comment: The impairment of short term memory (STM) involves forgetting information to which the subject has been recently exposed. An individual with signs of losing STM, indeed, asks for the same questions repeatedly, forgets where he just put something, forgets recent events or something he saw or read recently. The loss of immediate memory is also termed as fixation amnesia. Practically, STM works as a kind of scratchpad for temporary recall of a limited number of data that come from the sensory register and are ready to be processed through attention and recognition. On the other side, information collected in the long term memory (LTM) storage consist of memories for the performance of actions or skills (i.e., procedural memories, knowing how) and memories of facts, rules, concepts, and events (i.e., declarative memories, knowing that).
synonym: "Short term memory loss" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002354 ! Memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:47:14Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033688
name: Long term memory impairment
def: "A deficit in the ability to retrieve information from long-term memory, which can be defined as a seemingly unlimited capacity to store memories can last years and relate to the performance of actions or skills (i.e., procedural memories, knowing how) and memories of facts, rules, concepts, and events (i.e., declarative memories, knowing that)." [PMID:18394484, PMID:31424720]
synonym: "Long term memory loss" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002354 ! Memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:48:25Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033689
name: Anterograde memory impairment
def: "The impaired ability to establish new long-term memories." [PMID:24041667]
synonym: "Anterograde amnesia" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033688 ! Long term memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:50:11Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033690
name: Retrograde memory impairment
def: "Inability to retrieve information from the long-term memory that was acquired before the onset of amnesia." [PMID:24041667]
synonym: "Retrograde amnesia" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033688 ! Long term memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:53:43Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033691
name: Procedural memory loss
def: "A reduction in the ability to retrieve information about how to perform activities, such as how to ride a bike or drive a car, how to perform activities of daily living, or how to play a musical instrument." []
synonym: "Impaired procedural memory" EXACT []
synonym: "Procedural memory deficit" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033690 ! Retrograde memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:55:09Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033692
name: Declarative memory loss
def: "Impaired ability to remember facts and events." [PMID:33524264]
synonym: "Impaired declarative memory" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033690 ! Retrograde memory impairment
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:56:17Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033693
name: Phantosmia
def: "Perception of an odor in the absence of any stimuli in the surrounding environment that could emit the odor." [PMID:28334095]
comment: Phantosmia has been documented in individuals with epileptic seizures, schizophrenia, depression, migraine, and otorhinolaryngology problems. Phantosmia occurs in a variety of clinical conditions, and its causes are yet unknown. Prior accounts have suggested that the phantosmic sensations originate either in the peripheral olfactory nervous system or in central brain regions, such as the amygdala and the orbital frontal cortex. Patients who experienced phantosmia following head trauma were characterized by left frontal atrophy, suggestive of a cortical origin. However, a patient study reported successfully resolving phantosmia in 7 out of 8 patients through excision of the olfactory epithelium, suggesting also peripheral olfactory system involvement.
synonym: "Phantom odor" EXACT []
synonym: "Phantom odour" EXACT uk_spelling []
synonym: "Phantom smell" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0004408 ! Abnormality of the sense of smell
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T22:57:41Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033694
name: Tactile hallucination
def: "Perception of touch without a tactile stimulus." []
comment: There are many different types of tactile hallucinations, and one common type is formication. Formication is the sensation of insects crawling over the skin, a term derived from the Latin word for ant, "formica.
synonym: "Tactile hallucinations" EXACT plural_form []
is_a: HP:5200214 ! Somatic hallucination
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7062-8192
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7941-2961
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9221-9226
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0183-1637
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0003-1785-7126
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0003-2291-6952
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4958-6025
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:00:31Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033695
name: Occupational disability
def: "This is a general term that denotes a reduced ability to perform the work that one performed prior to an illness, and may be related to pain, cognitive dysfunction, fatigue or other physical disabilities." []
comment: This term does not intend to include or exclude legal definitions of occupational disability. It is included in the HPO because inability to work is a frequent manifestation of disease comparable in some ways to impairment of activities of daily living.
is_a: HP:0025142 ! Constitutional symptom
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:02:06Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033696
name: Pseudo-chilblain
def: "Acral areas of erythema with vesicles or pustules. The lesions resemble chilblains and have purpuric areas, affecting hands and feet." [PMID:32348545, PMID:33479703]
comment: This condition is termed pseudo-chilblain because of the similar appearance of these lesions to chilblains. Pseudochilblains may be observed in persons with COVID-19 without documented exposure to cold or other risk factors for chilblains.
synonym: "Chilblain-like lesion" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0011123 ! Inflammatory abnormality of the skin
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:03:37Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033697
name: Vesicular eruption
def: "A type of acute-onset skin rash characterized by multiple vesicles, which are circumscribed, fluid-containing, epidermal elevation generally considered less than 10mm in diameter at the widest point. The fluid contained in a vesicle may be clear, serous, or hemorrhagic." [PMID:33008676]
is_a: HP:0000988 ! Skin rash
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:06:54Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033698
name: Monomorphic vesicular eruption
def: "A type of vesicular eruption in which the vesicles are at same stages." [PMID:32348545]
comment: Monomorphic vesicular eruptions should be differentiated from polymorphic vesicular eruptions such as those seem in chickenpox.
is_a: HP:0033697 ! Vesicular eruption
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:07:55Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033699
name: Polymorphic vesicular eruption
def: "A type of vesicular eruption in which the vesicles are at different stages." []
comment: This feature can be seen in many diseases including chickenpox.
is_a: HP:0033697 ! Vesicular eruption
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:09:26Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033700
name: Papulovesicular eruption
def: "An acute onset rash characterized by by multiple vesicles, which are circumscribed, fluid-containing, epidermal elevations with a diameter less than 10mm at the widest point, and by multiple papules, which are circumscribed, solid epidermal elevations with no visible fluid with a diameter less than 10mm at the widest point." [PMID:32579185]
synonym: "Papulo-vesicular eruption" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0000988 ! Skin rash
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-07T23:10:27Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033701
name: Cortical sclerosis of the iliac wing
def: "Increased density related to increased bone mass in the outermost layer (edge) of the iliac wing." [PMID:17404618]
comment: See Figure 3B of PMID:17404618.
is_a: HP:0011867 ! Abnormal iliac wing morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-12T12:07:04Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033702
name: Subpleural curvilinear line
def: "This finding is a thin curvilinear opacity, 1-3 mm in thickness, lying less than 1 cm from and parallel to the pleural surface. It corresponds to atelectasis of normal lung if seen in the dependent posteroinferior portion of lung of a patient in the supine position and is subsequently shown to disappear on CT sections acquired with the patient prone. It may also be encountered in patients with pulmonary edema or fibrosis (other signs are usually present)." [PMID:18195376]
comment: Though described in the context of asbestosis, subpleural curvilinear line is not specific for asbestosis. See Figure 68 of Hansell DM, et al., Fleischner Society: glossary of terms for thoracic imaging. Radiology. 2008;246:697-722.
is_a: HP:0031457 ! Pulmonary opacity
is_a: HP:0031630 ! Abnormal subpleural morphology
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-12T12:28:43Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033703
name: Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor
def: "Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor (DNT) is a benign glioneuronal neoplasm that most commonly occurs in children and young adults and may present with medically intractable, chronic seizures. Lesions vary in size from 10 to 25 mm, although occasionally larger tumors of up to 70 mm have been reported. Grossly, tumors appear as well-defined, solitary nodular masses or poorly demarcated lesions. On the cut section, most tumors are cortically located and may extend into the underlying subcortical white matter in larger tumors. Multi-nodular appearance or cystic changes are commonly found" [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1735-8178, PMID:25408925, PMID:26493957]
comment: They are commonly found in the temporal lobe and may be associated with temporal lobe epilepsy. DNETs are sometimes found in association with focal cortical dysplasia and have both neuronal and glial features.
synonym: "DNET" EXACT abbreviation []
synonym: "DNT" EXACT abbreviation []
is_a: HP:0025170 ! Neuronal/glioneuronal neoplasm of the central nervous system
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-12T12:40:15Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033704
name: Elevated urinary homogentisic acid
def: "An increased amount of homogentisic acid in the urine." [PMID:32809406]
comment: This finding is characteristic for alkaptonuria.
is_a: HP:0033303 ! Elevated urinary monocarboxylic acid level
property_value: terms:creator https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2021-03-12T12:47:05Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:0033705
name: Tearfulness
def: "A feeling of sadness characterized by episodes of crying that can occur suddenly and are not easily controlled in social situations." []
synonym: "Crying episodes" EXACT []
