[Term]
id: HP:6000055
name: Exposure to aquatic animals
def: "History of exposure to aquatic animals." [PMID:29493404]
comment: Mycobacterium marinum is an opportunistic pathogen inducing infection in fresh and marine water fish. Human infections with Mycobacterium marinum have been associated with the exposure of damaged skin to polluted water from fish pools or contacting objects contaminated with infected fish. Fish mycobacteriosis is clinically manifested and characterized in man by purple painless nodules, liable to develop into superficial crusting ulceration with scar formation.
is_a: HP:4000110 ! Exposure history
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/10000
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2023-12-30T09:25:04Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6000056
name: Superficial blush-like subcutaneous fatty lesions
def: "Multiple, oblong, fatty lesions in the superficial subcutaneous fatty tissue. They are mostly under 2 cm in long axis diameter. They demonstrate nodular (blush-like) increased fluid signal at unenhanced MRI and are markedly hyperechoic at ultrasound." [PMID:23809264]
comment: These lesions are characteristicv of Adiposis dolorosa (Dercum's disease).
is_a: HP:0001001 ! Abnormality of subcutaneous fat tissue
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/10001
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2023-12-30T09:25:04Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6000057
name: Dermal multinucleated giant cells
def: "Presence of giant cells in the dermis. Giant cells are formed by fusion of various cells such as macrophage, epithelioid cells, monocytes, etc., These are multi-nucleated, large in size, and generally present at the site of chronic inflammation and other granulomatous conditions." [PMID:25284855, PMID:35478968]
synonym: "Multinucleated giant cells in skin" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0034924 ! Abnormal dermis morphology
