property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-12T07:34:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001296
name: Osteochondral lesion of talus
def: "Osteochondral lesion of the talus (OLT) is an injury involving the articular cartilage and subchondral bone, defined as any damage involving both articular cartilage and subchondral bone of the talar dome. OLT can cause ankle joint pain and can sometimes lead to instability and manifest as an area of abnormal, damaged cartilage and bone on the top of the talus bone (the lower bone of the ankle joint)." [PMID:30498800, PMID:34386361]
is_a: HP:0008365 ! Abnormal talus morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11205
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-12T07:34:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001297
name: Mallet toe
def: "A mallet toe involves the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint [such that] the distal phalanx is flexed on the middle phalanx." [PMID:32211125]
comment: The etiology of mallet toe is varied, and has been attributed to anatomy and toe length, shoewear, trauma, or a factor in systemic neurologic disease. With a fixed mallet toe deformity, patients often complain of pain at the dorsal DIP joint, and/or pain at the tip of the affected toe, where undue pressure from footwear and weight bearing is experienced. Calluses may be found at either of these locations. When symptoms can no longer be tolerated with treatments such as modified shoewear and padding in a patient without underlying systemic disease, surgical intervention may be indicated.
is_a: HP:0034398 ! Toe deformity
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11196
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-12T07:34:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001298
name: Bunionette deformity
def: "The bunionette, or tailor's bunion, is a lateral prominence of the fifth metatarsal head. Most commonly, bunionettes are the result of a widened 4-5 intermetatarsal angle with associated varus of the metatarsophalangeal joint." [PMID:17478753]
comment: Radiologically, foot radiographs showing varus deformity at the 5th metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint as measured by increased 4th-5th intermetatarsal angle (Imore than 8 degrees), increased lateral deviation angle (more that 7 degrees), or increased width of 5th metatarsal head (more than 14mm).
synonym: "Bunionette toe deformity" EXACT []
synonym: "Tailor's bunion" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0008089 ! Abnormality of the fifth metatarsal bone
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11197
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-12T07:34:50Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001299
name: Elevated CSF beta chorionic gonadotropin concentration
def: "The concentration of beta chorionic gonadotropin in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:11007160]
comment: CSF is marker for monitoring tumor recurrence or evaluation of treatment results in individuals with malignant germ cell tumor.
is_a: HP:0025456 ! Abnormal CSF protein concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/10862
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-31T17:29:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001300
name: Pigment epithelium detachment
def: "Separation of the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) from the underlying Bruch membrane." [PMID:28280989]
comment: Pigment epithelial detachment (PED) is a common feature in patients with exudative age-related macular degeneration. The prevalence of a PED has been reported to be 10-64% in eyes with polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy, and 10-22% in eyes with typical exudative AMD with choroidal neovascularization. The natural course is characterised by enlargement of the PED with consecutive regression and atrophy of the RPE and outer retina in avascular PEDs or formation of a disciform scar in vascularized PEDs.
is_a: HP:0007703 ! Abnormal retinal pigmentation
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/10868
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9042-0508
property_value: terms:date "2025-08-31T17:29:42Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001301
name: Retinal gliosis
def: "A pathological condition characterized by the proliferation of glial cells in the retina, often occurring as a reactive response to injury, inflammation, or degenerative changes, and typically resulting in structural and functional alterations in the retina." [PMID:26175331]
is_a: HP:0000546 ! Retinal degeneration
