[Term]
id: HP:6001458
name: Glenohumeral arthritis
def: "Anteropostrior (AP), axillary, or Grashey (true AP) view X-ray of the shoulder showing radiographic evidence of osteoarthritis such as joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, subchondral cysts, and/or osteophyte formation in the glenohumeral joint." [PMID:32662351]
synonym: "Shoulder arthritis" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0003043 ! Abnormal shoulder morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11005
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199
property_value: terms:date "2025-12-18T15:49:31Z" xsd:dateTime

[Term]
id: HP:6001459
name: Dental root fusion
def: "An anomaly of the roots of a multi-rooted tooth (such as a molar) in which the roots are not physically divided by bone or periodontal ligament space." [PMID:24862720]
comment: Root fusion may be formed either by the combining of roots because of cementum deposition with time or as a result of the failure of the Hertwig epithelial root sheath to develop or fuse in the furcation area, which is frequently found in maxillary and mandibular premolars and molars. In mandibular molars, root fusion usually presents as a C-shaped root, whereas in maxillary molars fused roots may show various root shapes, such as two or more roots being fused partially or completely.
synonym: "Fused dental root" EXACT []
synonym: "Unseparated dental root" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0006486 ! Abnormal dental root morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11427
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001460
name: Difficulty floating in water
def: "A phenomenon whereby an individual struggles to float, cannot swim despite adequate training, or simply sinks. This phenomenon may be observed in individuals with conditions such as osteopetrosis that are characterized by substantially increased bone mineral density (which implies reduced bouyancy)." [PMID:21455762]
is_a: HP:0025142 ! Constitutional symptom
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11434
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001461
name: Aortitis
def: "Aortitis refers to inflammatory changes to the wall of the aorta and incorporates both infectious and non-infectious etiologies." [PMID:27546992, PMID:33593995]
comment: The term aortitis encompasses inflammatory changes to the aortic wall from varying etiologies that are associated with certain specific histopathological lesions. The inflammatory response may be limited to the aortic wall or accompanied by changes directly adjacent to the vessel (e.g. retroperitoneal fibrosis) or other vessels (e.g., giant cell arteritis). A distinction is made between infectious and non-infectious aortitis. Takayasu arteritis and giant cell arteritis are the most common causes of non-infectious aortitis.
is_a: HP:0001679 ! Abnormal aortic morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11448
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5705-7831

[Term]
id: HP:6001462
name: Upper extremity claudication
def: "Pain in an arm that is triggered by muscular exertion." [PMID:21250079, PMID:37021621]
comment: This anomaly is observed in the setting of a patient with axillary or subclavian artery disease.
is_a: HP:0004417 ! Intermittent claudication
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11449
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001463
name: Tongue claudication
def: "Pain in the tongue induced by speaking or chewing." [PMID:30385998]
synonym: "Lingual claudication" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0004417 ! Intermittent claudication
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11450
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001464
name: Cerebral perivascular enhancement pattern
def: "Focal, regional or diffuse magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) enhancement of intracranial perivascular spaces (PVS). This enhancement pattern is recognizable by its topography that follows PVS distribution, particularly identifiable in regions where PVS predominate (centra semiovalia, basal ganglia and the brachium pontis)15 although it can be found throughout the whole brain. Depending on the perpendicular or parallel orientation of MRI sections to the long axis of PVS, enhanced PVS appear as dots or curvilinear elements respectively. Depending on the causal pathology, PVE may appear more linear or nodular." [PMID:39828213]
comment: Perivascular spaces (PVS) are fluid-filled structures that form the immediate peripheral environment of small cerebral vessels. They are a central component of the glymphatic system, which plays a crucial role in maintaining cerebral homeostasis.
synonym: "Perivascular enhancement pattern" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0100659 ! Abnormal cerebral vascular morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11303
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001465
name: Long head of biceps tendon dislocation
def: "Dislocation or subluxation of the long head of the biceps tendon out of the bicipital groove (as documented by magnetic resonance imaging of the shoulder) or dynamic ultrasound of the shoulder showing subluxation or dislocation of the long head of the biceps tendon out of the bicipital groove." [PMID:17126231, PMID:30475566]
comment: Isolated dislocation of the long head of the biceps is a rare entity. In most cases, dislocation of the biceps tendon out of the bicipital groove is usually associated with tears of the subscapularis tendon or massive rotator cuff tears (or both).
synonym: "LHBT dislocation" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0100261 ! Abnormal tendon morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11006
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001466
name: Elevated circulating somatostatin concentration
def: "The concentration of somatostatin in the blood corculation is above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:15496230]
comment: Somatostatin is a 14 amino acid polypeptide whose principal function is to regulate growth hormone release from the pituitary. Elevated serum and synovial fluid somatostatin levels have been associated with inflammatory responses most notably in rheumatoid arthritis.
synonym: "Somatostatin high in blood" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0010876 ! Abnormal circulating protein concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11510
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001467
name: Lack of attached gingiva
def: "Absence of attached gingiva. The gingiva is subdivided into the nonattached free gingiva (which includes the gingival margin and the papillae) and the attached gingiva. The attached gingiva is tightly and unmovably bound to the periosteum via collagen type I fibers. The gingiva is keratinized and thicker than the mucosa to provide mechanical protection. The border between attached gingiva and mucosa constitutes the mucogingival junction. In vivo, it is sometimes visible as a white line . The oral mucosa is loosely connected to the periosteum and nonkeratinized. It is movable, thin, more fragile, translucent, and blood vessels are visible." [PMID:33005042, PMID:41489249]
is_a: HP:0000168 ! Abnormal gingiva morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11517
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001468
name: Spontaneous diaphragmatic rupture
def: "A full thickness tear of the diaphragm without significant trauma." [PMID:22524904, PMID:28785503, PMID:41356277]
comment: Spontaneous diaphragmatic rupture is a rare complication of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome in which abnormal collagen synthesis results in tissue fragility and weakness of the diaphragm, predisposing to full-thickness tearing without significant trauma, typically triggered by a sudden increase in intra-abdominal pressure such as coughing, vomiting, or physical exertion, and resulting in herniation of abdominal contents into the thoracic cavity.
is_a: HP:0000775 ! Abnormality of the diaphragm
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11520
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001469
name: Elevated alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient
def: "An increase in the difference between the oxygen concentration in the alveoli and arterial system above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:31424737]
comment: The A-a gradient, or the alveolar-arterial gradient, measures the difference between the oxygen concentration in the alveoli and arterial system. The A-a gradient has important clinical utility as it can help narrow the differential diagnosis for hypoxemia. The A-a gradient calculation is as follows: A-a Gradient = PAO2 - PaO2. In this equation, PAO2 represents alveolar oxygen pressure and PaO2 representing arterial oxygen pressure. The arterial oxygen pressure (PaO2) can be directly assessed with an arterial blood gas test (ABG) or estimated with a venous blood gas test (VBG). The alveolar oxygen pressure (PAO2) is not easily measured directly; instead, it is estimated using the alveolar gas equation. The A-a gradient may be increased in clinical conditions such as pneumonia, with poor diffusion of oxygen across the alveolar-capillary unit and thus lower oxygen levels in the arterial blood (because of the physical barrier within the alveoli, limiting the diffusion of oxygen into the capillaries).
is_a: HP:0500165 ! Abnormal blood oxygen level
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11015
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001470
name: Abnormal concentration of circulating metanephrines
def: "Adrenaline, noradrenaline and dopamine are degraded by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) to form their inactive metabolites metanephrine, normetanephrine and 3-methoxytyramine (3-MT). When there is an indication for biochemical testing, such as suspicion of pheochromocytoma, metanephrine and normetanephrine metabolites are measured in plasma or 24-hour urine. This term represents an elevation of the concentration of metanephrine and normetanephrine in the blood circulation above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:37300464]
synonym: "Metanephrines high in blood" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0003117 ! Abnormal circulating hormone concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11016
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001471
name: Elevated circulating 3-O-methyldopa concentration
def: "The concentration of 3-O-methyldopa in the blood circulation is above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:28100251]
comment: 3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD) is produced by the methylation of L-DOPA by catechol-O-methyltransferase. 3-OMD may be elevated in the plasma and cerebrospinal fluid of Parkinson's disease patients taking L-DOPA and it is also observed to be elevated in individuals with Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase deficiency.
synonym: "3-O-methyldopa elevated" EXACT []
synonym: "Elevated circulating 3-OMD concentration" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0010996 ! Abnormal circulating monocarboxylic acid concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11540
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001472
name: Elevated CSF 3-O-methyldopa concentration
def: "The concentration of 3-O-methyldopa in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:28100251]
comment: 3-O-methyldopa (3-OMD) is produced by the methylation of L-DOPA by catechol-O-methyltransferase. 3-OMD may be elevated in the plasma and cerebrospinal fluid of Parkinson's disease patients taking L-DOPA and it is also observed to be elevated in individuals with Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase deficiency.
is_a: HP:0025454 ! Abnormal CSF metabolite concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11540
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001473
name: Fixed fetal head flexion
def: "An unchanging bending forward of the fetal head in the uterus as visualized by prenatal sonography. This sign can be defined as a lack of change in this position during the 30 min of observation. Additionally, identical head flexion is observable several times at intervals." [PMID:27987045]
comment: This feature has been observed in fetuses with Multiple pterygium syndrome, lethal type.
is_a: HP:0034245 ! Fetal head anomaly
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001474
name: Abnormal rectosigmoid index
def: "The rectosigmoid (R/S) ratio, a measure comparing rectal to sigmoid colon diameter, is below the lower limit of normal on prenatal ultrasound. The rectosigmoid is normally the widest segment of the fetal colon. If the rectosigmoid is narrower than the ascending or transverse colon above it), this is called a reversed rectosigmoid ratio or abnormal rectosigmoid index. The RS ratio is calculated by dividing the transverse rectal diameter by the sigmoid diameter. An R/S ratio of 1 or more is considered normal." [PMID:17164511, PMID:17502185, PMID:25677577]
comment: An abnormal rectosigmoid ratio may indicate Hirschsprung disease.
synonym: "Reversed rectosigmoid ratio" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0034207 ! Abnormal fetal gastrointestinal system morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11568
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001475
name: Umbilical cord edema
def: "Visible edema (swelling) in a cord with a minimal cross sectional area of 1-3 cm2. This may result from an excessive accumulation of Wharton jelly, resulting in a thick or edematous umbilical cord." [PMID:41133498, PMID:806296]
is_a: HP:0010881 ! Abnormality of the umbilical cord
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11570
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001476
name: Fetal enterolithiasis
def: "Enterolithiasis (concretions or stones forming within the gastrointestinal tract) is a prenatal sonographic sign defined by hyperechogenic foci within the - often distended - fetal bowel." [PMID:30879001]
synonym: "Enterolith in a fetal ultrasound" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0034207 ! Abnormal fetal gastrointestinal system morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11571
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001477
name: Malignant cells in CSF
def: "The identification of malignant cells in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), generally by microscopic examination of the CSF." [PMID:27759807, PMID:573381]
synonym: "Cerebrospinal fluid cancer cells" EXACT []
synonym: "Cerebrospinal fluid neoplastic cells" EXACT []
synonym: "Positive CSF cytology" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002921 ! Abnormal cerebrospinal fluid morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11538
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001478
name: Greek warrior helmet sign
def: "A prenatal sonographic finding characterized by a high forehead, prominent glabella, and a broad, un-tapered nasal bridge that merges directly into the brow line. This appearance is said to resemble a Greek (Corinthian) warrior helmet because the wide, solid vertical column down the center of the face is said to resemble the fixed, protective nose guard of a Corinthian helmet." [PMID:15305099, PMID:16258951, PMID:17324995]
comment: This sign is suggestive of the Wolf-Hirschhorn (4p-) syndrome.
is_a: HP:0034245 ! Fetal head anomaly
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11573
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9947-035X

[Term]
id: HP:6001479
name: Somatostatinoma
def: "Somatostatinoma is a rare, functional neuroendocrine tumor (NET) that primarily arises in the pancreas and duodenum, characterized by excessive production of somatostatin." [PMID:31869077]
comment: A somatostatinoma secretes somatostatin, which typically inhibits numerous gastrointestinal and endocrine functions. Clinically, this condition often presents with a classic triad of diabetes mellitus, cholelithiasis, and steatorrhea, resulting from hormonal suppression. On noncontrast CT, somatostatinomas typically appear isodense relative to pancreatic parenchyma. Endoscopic ultrasound is highly sensitive and specific for detecting pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms, including lesions smaller than 5 mm. Somatostatin receptor-based positron emission tomography (PET) using appropriate radiolabeled ligands demonstrates excellent sensitivity and specificity for tumor localization and staging.
is_a: HP:0100634 ! Neuroendocrine neoplasm
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11510
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001480
name: Erythematous scalp plaque
def: "A circumscribed, palpable, potentially elevated, erythematous lesion > 1 cm in diameter and localized to the scalp. Erythematous plaques exhibiting erythema, i.e., abnormal redness of the skin due to localized dilation and congestion of superficial dermal capillaries, typically as a result of inflammation, irritation, infection, or vasodilation, and usually blanching with pressure." [PMID:26801523, PMID:37153943]
comment: Erythematous plaque of the scalp may be observed in dermatomyositis and often are pruritic.
is_a: HP:0025474 ! Erythematous plaque
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11582
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001481
name: Positive hair pull test
def: "An abnormal result of the hair pull test, in which the clinician selects 50 to 60 hairs and holds the bundle close to the scalp between the thumb, index finger, and long finger. The clinician then firmly pulls on the bundle using slow traction as the fingers slide down the hair shaft, avoiding a fast and forceful tug. The hair pulls are performed at the vertex, parietal areas, and the occipital area of the scalp. Next, the pulled hairs are counted. Any broken hairs that were extracted from the bundle during the pull are discarded. If more than 10 percent of the hairs in each bundle are removed from a scalp area, the hair pull test is considered positive." [PMID:28010890]
comment: If fewer than 10% are removed, then the hair loss can usually be attributed to normal shedding. If a test is positive in more than one scalp region, the clinician must consider telogen or anagen effluvium. Other hair disorders (ie, alopecia areata) may only have a positive hair pull test in the affected area. The hair pull test is ideally used for monitoring the advancing edge of alopecia areata, acute cases of telogen effluvium, anagen effluvium, and loose anagen syndrome.
is_a: HP:0100037 ! Abnormality of the scalp hair
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11464
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001482
name: Loss of acquired purposeful hand skills
def: "A type of neurodevelopmental regression characterized by deterioration or disappearance of previously acquired voluntary, goal-directed hand movements, such as reaching, grasping, manipulating objects, or self-feeding." [PMID:21154482]
synonym: "Loss of acquired hand function" EXACT []
synonym: "Loss of purposeful hand movements" EXACT []
synonym: "Loss of purposeful hand skills" EXACT []
synonym: "Loss of purposeful hand use" EXACT []
synonym: "Regression of purposeful hand use" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0033044 ! Motor regression
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11496
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001483
name: Loss of acquired spoken language
def: "A type of neurodevelopmental regression characterized by deterioration or disappearance of previously acquired expressive verbal communication, including words, phrases, or sentences the individual had used meaningfully, after these skills had been acquired during development." [PMID:21154482]
comment: Loss of acquired language is based on best acquired spoken language skill, not strictly on the acquisition of distinct words or higher language skills.
synonym: "Loss of expressive language" EXACT []
synonym: "Regression of spoken language" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0034332 ! Cognitive regression
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11496
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001484
name: Postnatal deceleration of headgrowth
def: "Slowing of the rate of head circumference growth occurring after birth, in an individual with normal head circumference at birth, leading to progressive deviation from normal growth curves and, in many cases, acquired (postnatal, secondary) microcephaly." [PMID:21154482]
comment: The human brain undergoes dramatic changes in size and connectivity after birth. From birth to age six years, the brain increases in size by four-fold, reaching 90 percent of adult volume. During normal development, the growth of head circumference is largely driven by growth of underlying brain tissue.
is_a: HP:0000240 ! Abnormality of skull size
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11496
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001485
name: Foot small muscle atrophy
def: "Wasting of the small muscles of the foot such as the abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, and the interossei muscles." [PMID:15920063]
comment: This sign may indicate diabetix neuropathy of length-dependent polyneuropathies such as Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease.
is_a: HP:0008944 ! Distal lower limb amyotrophy
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11499
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001486
name: Abnormal gag reflex
def: "An abnormality of the gag reflex (also known as the pharyngeal reflex)." [PMID:32119389]
comment: The gag reflex is an involuntary reflex involving bilateral pharyngeal muscle contraction and elevation of the soft palate. This reflex may be evoked by stimulation of the posterior pharyngeal wall, tonsillar area, or tongue base. The gag reflex is believed to be an evolutionary reflex that developed as a method to prevent swallowing foreign objects and prevent choking.
is_a: HP:0031826 ! Abnormal reflex
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001487
name: Abnormal lingual nerve physiology
def: "A functional abnormality of the lingual nerve, which conveys general sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and the floor of the mouth." [PMID:31536258]
comment: The lingual nerve is a sensory nerve that arises from the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V).
is_a: HP:0031911 ! Abnormal fifth cranial nerve physiology
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001488
name: Abnormal vulvar morphology
def: "Any structural abnormality of the vulva, the external female genitalia comprising the mons pubis, labia, clitoris, and vaginal vestibule." []
synonym: "Abnormal vulva morphology" EXACT []
synonym: "Abnormality of the vulva" EXACT []
synonym: "Vulvar malformation" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0000055 ! Abnormal female external genitalia morphology
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001489
name: Any structural anomaly of the neck region
def: "Any structural anomaly of the trapezius muscle, the large superficial back muscle that resembles a trapezoid. It extends from the external protuberance of the occipital bone to the lower thoracic vertebrae and laterally to the spine of the scapula." [PMID:30085536]
comment: The trapezius has upper, middle, and lower groups of fibers. The ventral rami of C3 and C4 innervate the sensory functions of the trapezius. The spinal accessory nerve (cranial nerve XI) innervates the motor function of the trapezius. The function of the trapezius is to stabilize and move the scapula.
is_a: HP:0011006 ! Abnormal morphology of the musculature of the neck
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001490
name: Trapezius asymmetry
def: "A difference in the size, shape, or bulk of the trapezius muscles between the left and right side." []
is_a: HP:0011006 ! Abnormal morphology of the musculature of the neck
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001491
name: Axillary hyperpigmentation
def: "An abnormal increase in skin coloration affecting one or both axillae, resulting in the affected skin appearing darker than the surrounding skin." [PMID:40843925]
comment: Hyperpigmentation in the axillary is more common than in other parts of the skin because of irritation and inflammation usually caused by body folds, friction, hair plucking, shaving, deodorants, and using whitening agents. Axillary hyperpigmentation may manifest as hyperpigmented macules or plaques, which can affect the dermis, epidermis, or both.
synonym: "Dark axillae" EXACT []
synonym: "Darkened underarms" EXACT []
synonym: "Underarm hyperpigmentation" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0000953 ! Hyperpigmentation of the skin
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001492
name: Aberrant tympanic bony island
def: "Ectopic or heterotopic mature osseous tissue localized within the fibrous layers or structural boundaries of the tympanic membrane, independent of normal middle ear ossicular anatomy." [PMID:27428025]
comment: This feature may be observed in Fanconi Anemia and may be associated with hearing impairment.
synonym: "Bony island of the tympanic membrane" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0040090 ! Abnormal tympanic membrane morphology
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001493
name: Cranial exostosis
def: "A benign bony outgrowth projecting from the outer surface of one or more of the cranial bones." [PMID:20043756, PMID:28614150]
synonym: "Cranial exostoses" EXACT []
synonym: "Skull exostosis" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0100777 ! Exostoses
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001494
name: Cubital dimple
def: "A cutaneous indentation (dimple) located in the cubital fossa, which is a triangular depression located on the anterior aspect of the elbow." []
synonym: "Antecubital dimple" EXACT []
