[Term]
id: HP:6001530
name: Overfaced vertebral pedicle
def: "An abnormal orientation of the vertebral pedicle. The vertebral arch is formed by pedicles and laminae. Two pedicles extend from the sides of the vertebral body to join the body to the arch. An overfaced vertebral pedicle is angled more medially than normal, which implies that the the lateral border of the vertebrae appears outside the lateral edge of the pedicles on the frontal view on spine radiography." [PMID:37053103]
synonym: "Overfaced vertebral pedicles" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0030277 ! Abnormal vertebral pedicle morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11699
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001531
name: Decreased circulating pepsinogen I concentration
def: "The concentration of pepsinogen I in the blood circulation is below the lower limit of normal." [PMID:26556485]
comment: Human pepsinogens are proenzymes for pepsin, a digestive enzyme produced by gastric chief cells. Human pepsinogens are biochemically and immunochemically classified into two groups: pepsinogen I and pepsinogen II. Serum pepsinogen (SPG) may function as a marker of the functional and morphologic status of the gastric mucosa, including atrophic changes and inflammation, such as H. pylori infection, AG and IM. Serum PGI and PGII levels are increased with increasing severity of H. pylori-related chronic gastritis. However, when atrophic changes in the corpus are accompanied by a loss of cells in the corpus, including those secreting PGI, the level of PGI decreases, whereas the level of PGII remains high or stable.
is_a: HP:0010876 ! Abnormal circulating protein concentration
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11650
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001532
name: Displaced radial artery
def: "An abnormal position of the radial artery, displaced from its normal anatomical location." [PMID:10816398]
is_a: HP:0031640 ! Abnormal radial artery morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11653
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001533
name: Abnormal body image
def: "A distortion in the perception, evaluation, or experience of one's own body, resulting in a body image that is inaccurate, distorted, or excessively influenced by body shape or weight." [PMID:38102717]
comment: Body image is the intuitive portrayal a person holds of their body, regardless of their appearance. Body image is, thus, a complex concept including self-observations, cognitions, emotions, memories, fantasies as well as behaviors related to an individual's body, both conscious and unconscious. Body image is how an individual considers or imagines their body and how it appears to others. Gender and social standards might influence the ideal physique. A person has a poor body image when they are preoccupied with the fictitious flaws of their physical appearance.
synonym: "Body image disturbance" EXACT []
synonym: "Distorted body image" EXACT []
synonym: "Disturbed body image" EXACT []
is_a: HP:5200242 ! Cognitive distortion
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11654
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001534
name: Limited thumb abduction
def: "A reduced range of motion of thumb abduction (movement of the thumb away from the plane of the palm), as distinct from a fixed adduction contracture or a resting adducted-thumb position." []
synonym: "Reduced thumb abduction" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0001376 ! Limitation of joint mobility
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11664
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001535
name: Oral mucosal fragility
def: "Increased fragility of the oral mucous membranes, such that the tissue tears or bleeds easily with minimal mechanical trauma." [PMID:41883891]
synonym: "Friable oral mucosa" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0011830 ! Abnormal oral mucosa morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11666
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0001-4998-8617

[Term]
id: HP:6001536
name: Anti-complement component antibody positivity
def: "The presence of an antibody in the blood circulation that is directed against a component of the complement system." [PMID:23790637]
comment: The complement system is an important part of the immune defense. Traditionally placed in the innate immune system, complement plays an essential role in the host's defense against pathogens. More recent insight clearly indicates that complement components also play essential roles in instructing the adaptive immune response.
is_a: HP:0030057 ! Autoimmune antibody positivity
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11620
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001537
name: Anti-factor B antibody positivity
def: "The presence of an antibody in the blood circulation that is directed against factor B, a component of the alternative C3 convertase." [PMID:23790637, PMID:32034108, PMID:38252289]
comment: Anti-factor B antibodies may be observed in postinfectious glomerulonephritis, C3 glomerulopathy, and immune-complex membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis.
synonym: "Anti-FB antibody positivity" EXACT []
is_a: HP:6001536 ! Anti-complement component antibody positivity
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11620
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3387-1836

[Term]
id: HP:6001538
name: Anti-ribosomal P protein antibody positivity
def: "The presence of an antibody in the blood circulation that is directed against ribosomal P protein." [PMID:15804709, PMID:34796669, PMID:37206021, PMID:37572300]
comment: Anti-ribosomal P protein antibodies (anti-ribo P) have been reported as one of the specific autoantibodies in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) and has been demonstrated to bind and activate macrophages in vitro. Anti-ribosomal P protein antibodies (Abs) recognize three specific ribosomal proteins located in the large ribosome's subunit. The term anti-ribosomal P protein Abs, often shortened in anti-P Abs, is due to the fact that these three proteins, P0, P1 and P2, of 38, 19 and 17 Kd molecular weight, respectively, are phosphorylated. One of the major points of interest of these autoAbs derives from their high specificity for systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), their description in other connective tissue diseases (CTDs) being only occasional.
is_a: HP:0030057 ! Autoimmune antibody positivity
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11622
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3387-1836

[Term]
id: HP:6001539
name: Anti-DFS70 antibody positivity
def: "The presence of autoantibodies (immunoglobulins) in the blood circulation that react against DFS70." [PMID:23304189]
comment: DFS70 IS a nuclear protein called DFS70 (also known as LEDGF/p75, lens epithelium-derived growth factor), which is named for the dense fine speckled (DFS) pattern it produces on HEp-2 indirect immunofluorescence. Anti-DFS70 antibodies have been found in the sera of patients with a variety of conditions including cancer, and even more interestingly in healthy individuals. Anti-DFS70 antibodies are common among ANA-positive individuals with no evidence of systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases. Among autoimmune patients with this autoantibody over a half had evidence of autoimmune thyroiditis.
is_a: HP:0030057 ! Autoimmune antibody positivity
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11638
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001540
name: Premature patella ossification
def: "Ossification of the patella is detected earlier than the age range typical for the general population." []
is_a: HP:0003045 ! Abnormal patella morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11639
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001541
name: Erysipelas-like erythema
def: "A red, warm and tender rash, usually involving the distal part of the shin, dorsum of the foot or ankle, which typically fades, within 24 to 72 hours. A typical lesion of erysipeloid erythema is comprised of an erythematous plaque with well-defined borders located on the lower extremity. Histologically one may expect a perivascular and interstitial inflammatory infiltrate containing neutrophils and nuclear dust." [PMID:22243424]
synonym: "Erysipeloid erythema" EXACT []
synonym: "Pseudoerysipelas" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0010783 ! Erythema
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11640
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001542
name: Elevated urinary biotin level
def: "The amount of biotin in the urine, normalized for urine concentration, is above the upper limit of normal." [PMID:42335246]
comment: This feature may be seen in TPK1-related thiamine metabolism dysfunction syndrome.
synonym: "Biotin high in urine" EXACT []
is_a: HP:6000531 ! Abnormal urinary organic compound level
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11641
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001543
name: Second-wind phenomenon
def: "The second-wind phenomenon occurs approximately 10 minutes after the onset of exertion, denotes a sudden and significant improvement in aerobic exercise tolerance after a brief rest period, following the development of muscle stiffness and myalgias, enabling the person to continue physical activity with a resolution of symptoms triggered at the start of exertion." [PMID:20301518, PMID:39318660]
comment: The second wind phenomenon is experienced by individuals with McArdle's disease and can be explained by increased blood flow, optimized delivery of free fatty acids with concurrent activation of their metabolism, and increased utilization of glucose. However, pre-exercise glucose intake can prevent the onset of this phenomenon.
is_a: HP:0011804 ! Abnormal muscle physiology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11647
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0005-6714-5727

[Term]
id: HP:6001544
name: Small intestinal porcelain-white serosal plaque
def: "Yellow plaques, said to resemble porcelain and located in the small intestinal serosa." [PMID:35443671]
comment: The disease in the gut is characterized by a purely arteriopathic and extravascular sclerosing process confined to the subserosal fat. The small- and medium-sized blood vessels within the subserosal fat exhibit an expansile fibrosing neointimal arteriopathy eventuating in acellular collagen plugs.
is_a: HP:0025129 ! Abnormal small intestinal mucosa morphology
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11651
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001545
name: Dysplastic Liver Nodule
def: "A precancerous lesion in which hepatocytes show morphologic alterations and, depending on the degree of atypia." []
synonym: "Dysplastic hepatic nodule" EXACT []
is_a: HP:0002896 ! Neoplasm of the liver
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11697
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199

[Term]
id: HP:6001546
name: Self-harm ideation
def: "Self-harm can be broadly defined as self-injurious behaviour irrespective of motive; self-harm is a broad term that can include non-suicidal self-injury (self-injurious behaviour without intent to die). Self-harm ideation therefore refers to thoughts of self-harm without self-injurious behaviour." [PMID:40991674]
is_a: HP:0025785 ! Abnormal preoccupation
property_value: IAO:0000233 https://github.com/obophenotype/human-phenotype-ontology/issues/11708
property_value: terms:contributor https://orcid.org/0009-0002-1118-4784

